---
<section class="app-page">
  <div class="app-prose">

  {% include "chrome/admin-sections.njk" %}

  <h1 class="text-3xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-2">Analytics</h1>
  <p class="text-brand-muted mb-8 max-w-prose">
    Who reads the public site, what they read, and how they got there, and the same for the app.
    Counted on my own server. A row holds a path, which of the two sites it was on, the host a reader
    arrived from, the time, a coarse browser, OS and device, and a visitor number that one browser
    keeps for 24 hours and that is erased from the row after 30 days. No address, no cookie, no
    User-Agent string.
  </p>

  {# Two controls, and everything below is drawn from the one answer they fetch together - so
     nothing on this page can be showing a different window, or a different property, from
     anything else on it. The property filter is a real parameter rather than a filter applied
     here: a visit that crossed from the site to the app is two visits in two properties, so a
     total filtered in the browser would be right for the lists and wrong for every rate. #}
  <div class="mb-6 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-x-4 gap-y-2">
    <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
      <label for="range" class="text-sm text-brand-muted">Last</label>
      <select id="range" class="field w-auto py-1.5 text-sm">
        <option value="7">7 days</option>
        <option value="30" selected>30 days</option>
        <option value="90">90 days</option>
        <option value="365">365 days</option>
      </select>
    </div>
    <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
      <label for="property" class="text-sm text-brand-muted">Site</label>
      <select id="property" class="field w-auto py-1.5 text-sm">
        <option value="all" selected>Both</option>
        <option value="site">Public site</option>
        <option value="app">App</option>
      </select>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div id="status" role="status" aria-live="polite" class="mb-6 text-sm"></div>

  {# Every figure starts as an em dash marked `data-loading`, never as a zero. A zero is a
     measurement and these have not been measured yet; the dash is the page saying it does not
     know. The same rule is what the empty window does below: "nothing yet" is an answer, and a
     nought drawn where no counting has happened is an invention. #}
  <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 mb-4">
    <div class="stat-card">
      <span class="stat-label">Visitors</span>
      <span class="stat-number" id="visitors" data-loading>—</span>
      <span class="stat-note" id="visitorsNote">Browsers, counted once a day each.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-card">
      <span class="stat-label">Visits</span>
      <span class="stat-number" id="visits" data-loading>—</span>
      <span class="stat-note" id="visitsNote">A gap of 30 minutes starts a new one.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-card">
      <span class="stat-label">Views</span>
      <span class="stat-number" id="views" data-loading>—</span>
      <span class="stat-note" id="viewsNote">In this window.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-card">
      <span class="stat-label">Right now</span>
      <span class="stat-number" id="live" data-loading>—</span>
      <span class="stat-note" id="liveNote">The last five minutes, whatever the window says.</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4 mb-6">
    <div class="stat-card">
      <span class="stat-label">Views per visit</span>
      <span class="stat-number" id="perVisit" data-loading>—</span>
      <span class="stat-note" id="perVisitNote">Pages read before leaving.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-card">
      <span class="stat-label">Bounce rate</span>
      <span class="stat-number" id="bounce" data-loading>—</span>
      <span class="stat-note" id="bounceNote">Visits that read one page.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-card">
      <span class="stat-label">Average visit</span>
      <span class="stat-number" id="duration" data-loading>—</span>
      <span class="stat-note" id="durationNote">Between the first and last page.</span>
    </div>
    <div class="stat-card">
      <span class="stat-label">From LinkedIn</span>
      <span class="stat-number" id="linkedin" data-loading>—</span>
      <span class="stat-note" id="linkedinNote">Views whose referrer was a LinkedIn domain.</span>
    </div>
  </div>

  {# The five things about these numbers that are artefacts of how they are made rather than
     facts about readers. Written on the page and not only in the code, because a dashboard that
     prints "average visit 41s" beside "bounce 62%" without saying the second one produced the
     first is a dashboard that misleads its only reader. #}
  <details class="card mb-10">
    <summary class="cursor-pointer text-sm font-semibold text-brand-text">
      What these figures cannot tell you
    </summary>
    <ul class="mt-3 space-y-2 text-sm text-brand-muted">
      <li><strong class="text-brand-text">A bounce lasts zero seconds, by arithmetic.</strong>
        The clock runs between two page views, so the last page of every visit is untimed and a
        one-page visit is untimed entirely. Every bounce therefore averages in as nought, which is
        most of why the average visit looks short. Read the two figures together or neither.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-brand-text">A visit that began before the window is cut at its
        edge.</strong> Its earlier pages are outside the range and the query cannot see them, so
        the first visit of any window reads shorter and thinner than it was.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-brand-text">The same reader on both sites is two visitors.</strong>
        The number is computed per property on purpose - two sites, two consent regimes - so
        signing in from the public site is counted as arriving somewhere new. That is a choice,
        not a measurement.</li>
      <li><strong class="text-brand-text">"Visitors" means browsers.</strong> One person on a
        phone and a laptop is two, and the same browser tomorrow is two more. Every analytics
        product means this by the word; none of them says so.</li>
      <li id="unidentifiedNote"></li>
    </ul>
  </details>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-1">Views per day</h2>
  <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mb-4 max-w-prose">
    Every day in the range, including the ones with nothing on them.
  </p>
  <div id="chart" class="card mb-10">
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted">Loading…</p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-1">When it is read</h2>
  <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mb-4 max-w-prose">
    Day of the week against hour, in UTC - the same clock every other figure here uses, so a
    reader in another zone should add their own offset rather than trust the column heading.
  </p>
  <div id="heatmap" class="card mb-10">
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted">Loading…</p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-1">What they are reading on</h2>
  <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mb-4 max-w-prose">
    Four coarse facts, each from a short closed list. The browser's own version string is never
    stored and neither is the exact window width; what is kept is the name and the bracket.
  </p>
  <div id="agents" class="mb-10">
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted">Loading…</p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-1">Which pages are read</h2>
  <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mb-4 max-w-prose">
    Split by property, because "which of the two sites" is the first question and a merged list
    of paths cannot answer it.
  </p>
  <div id="paths" class="mb-10">
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted">Loading…</p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-1">Where visits start and end</h2>
  <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mb-4 max-w-prose">
    Not the same question as the busiest pages: a page can be read constantly and entered almost
    never. These count visits, not views.
  </p>
  <div id="journeys" class="mb-10">
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted">Loading…</p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-1">Where readers came from</h2>
  <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mb-4 max-w-prose">
    The host only. A full referring URL can carry somebody's search terms in its query string, so
    the API keeps the host and throws the rest away before anything is written down.
  </p>
  <div id="referrers" class="mb-10">
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted">Loading…</p>
  </div>

  <h2 class="text-xl font-bold text-brand-text mb-1">The last twenty visits</h2>
  <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted mb-4 max-w-prose">
    The one thing here that is not an aggregate. It carries no visitor number: what a visit read
    and for how long is the question, and the handle that joins two visits together is not part
    of it.
  </p>
  <div id="recent">
    <p class="text-sm text-brand-muted">Loading…</p>
  </div>

</div>

</section>
